// A Read is permitted if there was formerly a Login. This policy writes the
// pinned field with EXACTLY the value the schema pins it to
// (`callerPrincipal: principal`). The pin is still injected, so the
// predicate constrains `callerPrincipal` twice against the same request
// value — a redundant no-op. The verdicts match case 1117 (which wrote
// nothing), demonstrating that an agreeing hand-written field neither relaxes
// nor tightens the pin.
//
// Positive/negative control across two traces:
//   trace_1: the prior Login is by the SAME principal as the Read → permitted.
//   trace_2: IDENTICAL except the prior Login is by a DIFFERENT principal →
//            the correlation (whether from the author's copy or the pin's)
//            forces a mismatch → NOT permitted.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: context.input.user, callerPrincipal: principal }
};
